<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jones Law (Philippines)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Philippine Autonomy Act, Jones Act (Philippines)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jones Law, or Philippine Autonomy Act, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> law of 1916 that replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-commission">Philippine Commission</a> with an elected Philippine Senate, creating an all-Filipino legislature. Its preamble stated that the United States would grant independence once a stable government was established. It served as the islands' basic law until the Tydings-McDuffie Act set up the Commonwealth in 1935.</p></div>
